00:00:53.276 --> 00:00:58.276
Dark matter with a slight electric charge or with the behavior of a tiny magnet can lose

00:00:58.276 --> 00:01:03.716
energy in collisions with ordinary matter and be captured by a planet or a star. Over

00:01:03.716 --> 00:01:08.316
billions of years the particles gather toward the center, and annihilations there could

00:01:08.316 --> 00:01:14.616
yield neutrinos, particles that rarely interact with ordinary matter or spare heat. Theoretical

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:20.616
calculations were set against existing observations of Earth, the Sun, Jupiter, and white dwarfs,

00:01:21.096 --> 00:01:26.416
the leftover cores of dead stars. Ice cubes' neutrino searches set limits for Earth and the

00:01:26.416 --> 00:01:31.496
sun, and heat output measured from Jupiter and from white dwarfs ruled out certain pairings

00:01:31.496 --> 00:01:32.636
of mass and interaction.

00:01:33.276 --> 00:01:38.196
Earth was the stronger test of a tiny electric charge, while white dwarfs boxed in other

00:01:38.196 --> 00:01:39.576
electromagnetic properties.

00:01:40.196 --> 00:01:45.756
Work in the Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics rules out some pairings of mass and properties

00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:47.996
that other experiments had still left open.

00:01:49.036 --> 00:01:54.496
Ringdowns and gravitational waves from black hole mergers, studied by more than 70 researchers

00:01:54.496 --> 00:02:00.076
with computer models and a literature review, universe today notes, match Einstein's theory

00:02:00.076 --> 00:02:05.516
of general relativity in hundreds of events. Gravitational waves are ripples from violent motions

00:02:05.516 --> 00:02:11.816
of huge masses, and ring down as the fading tone once two black holes have become one. Black hole

00:02:11.816 --> 00:02:17.476
spectroscopy reads that tone the way astronomers read light. The study takes that spectroscopy

00:02:17.476 --> 00:02:22.516
from a theoretical idea toward a practical tool by measuring the waves given off after the merger.

00:02:22.516 --> 00:02:28.116
However, current detectors still meet measurement limits, yet next-generation ground-based instruments

00:02:28.116 --> 00:02:33.476
in a classical and quantum gravity analysis are expected to deliver more precise data.

00:02:34.497 --> 00:02:40.377
Random direction gas falling onto early supermassive black holes, Fizz.org reports, keeps their

00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:40.917
spin low.

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:48.557
Low spin means only 6-7.5% of infalling mass turns into radiation instead of the usual 10%.

00:02:48.557 --> 00:02:54.077
shortening the growth time scale from 45 million years to roughly 27 to 32 million years.

00:02:54.537 --> 00:02:57.897
A quasar is a brilliant galactic core powered by a black hole.

00:02:58.497 --> 00:03:02.037
Redshift is a measure of how far back in cosmic time we are looking.

00:03:02.577 --> 00:03:06.677
The scenario reproduces observed masses of every supermassive black hole

00:03:06.677 --> 00:03:14.117
with a reliable measurement at redshift above 7, including the quasar J0313-1806,

00:03:14.117 --> 00:03:19.597
From seed holes of about 100 solar masses that appeared around 100 million years after

00:03:19.597 --> 00:03:25.057
the Big Bang, work in astronomy and astrophysics predicts that the most massive early black holes

00:03:25.057 --> 00:03:26.297
should be slowly spinning.

